//@ expect: accept
//@ reason: `byte` (unsigned 8-bit, lowers to C++ `unsigned char`) as a plain
//@ reason: VALUE type -- declaration, integer-literal assignment, arithmetic,
//@ reason: and a bitwise op. Added because plain `char` is SIGNED in
//@ reason: generated C++ on this project's toolchain: a byte value above 127
//@ reason: reads back negative, silently corrupting code that uses it as a
//@ reason: table index or in unsigned arithmetic (found while investigating
//@ reason: zlib-cling's compare256 SIMD shim). This fixture's value (200) is
//@ reason: specifically chosen to be > 127 -- proving the arithmetic result
//@ reason: stays unsigned/correct, not the sign-extended negative `char`
//@ reason: would silently produce. Printed via `int` locals (not the `byte`
//@ reason: values directly) because C++'s ostream has an `unsigned char`
//@ reason: overload that prints it as a raw CHARACTER, not a number -- a
//@ reason: real, confirmed C++ behavior this fixture works around the same
//@ reason: way any hand-written C++ using unsigned char must.
//@ run: 200 100

int main() {
    byte b = 200;
    byte shifted = b >> 1;
    int bAsInt = b + 0;
    int shiftedAsInt = shifted + 0;
    std::cout << bAsInt << " " << shiftedAsInt << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
